⚙️ ISP Router Configuration
Enable IPv6 Routing
R1(config)# ipv6 unicast-routing
Configure P2P Interface
R1(config)# interface g0/0
R1(config-if)# ipv6 address fd00:100::1/64
R1(config-if)# no shutdown
Configure Public IPv6 Loopback
R1(config)# interface loopback0
R1(config-if)# ipv6 address 2001:db8:1::1/128
⚙️ Customer Router Configuration
Enable IPv6 Routing
R2(config)# ipv6 unicast-routing
Configure P2P Interface
R2(config)# interface g0/0
R2(config-if)# ipv6 address fd00:100::2/64
R2(config-if)# no shutdown
Configure Public IPv6 Loopback
R2(config)# interface loopback0
R2(config-if)# ipv6 address 2001:db8:2::1/128
🛣️ Static Routing
Customer → ISP

The Customer router needs a route toward the ISP's public Loopback address.

R2(config)# ipv6 route 2001:db8:1::1/128 fd00:100::1

Meaning:

Destination:
2001:db8:1::1/128

Next-Hop:
fd00:100::1
ISP → Customer

The ISP router needs a route toward the Customer's public Loopback address.

R1(config)# ipv6 route 2001:db8:2::1/128 fd00:100::2

Meaning:

Destination:
2001:db8:2::1/128

Next-Hop:
fd00:100::2
🔄 Packet Flow

When the Customer router sends traffic toward the ISP's public IPv6:

R2 Loopback
2001:db8:2::1
       |
       | Routing Lookup
       ↓
fd00:100::1
       |
       ↓
ISP R1 G0/0
       |
       ↓
R1 Loopback
2001:db8:1::1

The important concept is:

The source and destination are Public IPv6 addresses, while the ISP–Customer transit network uses ULA IPv6 addresses.

🧪 Connectivity Testing
1. Verify IPv6 Interfaces
ISP
R1# show ipv6 interface brief
Customer
R2# show ipv6 interface brief

Expected interfaces:

G0/0       fd00:100::1/64
Loopback0  2001:db8:1::1/128

and:

G0/0       fd00:100::2/64
Loopback0  2001:db8:2::1/128
2. Test P2P Connectivity

From Customer:

R2# ping ipv6 fd00:100::1

Expected:

!!!!!

This confirms that the ISP–Customer IPv6 transit link is operational.

3. Test Public IPv6 Reachability

From Customer:

R2# ping ipv6 2001:db8:1::1

From ISP:

R1# ping ipv6 2001:db8:2::1

Both should successfully reach the remote Loopback.

🔍 Routing Verification

Check the IPv6 routing table:

R1# show ipv6 route

The ISP should have a static route similar to:

S  2001:DB8:2::1/128
   [1/0] via FD00:100::2

Customer:

R2# show ipv6 route

Expected:

S  2001:DB8:1::1/128
   [1/0] via FD00:100::1
🔎 Neighbor Discovery Verification

IPv6 does not use ARP.

Instead, IPv6 uses Neighbor Discovery Protocol (NDP) through ICMPv6.

Verify neighbors with:

R1# show ipv6 neighbors

and:

R2# show ipv6 neighbors

This helps verify Layer-2 neighbor discovery on the P2P link.

