🌐 Real-World ISP Perspective

In a production ISP environment, the architecture can become more hierarchical.

For example, an ISP may allocate a customer prefix such as:

2001:db8:2000::/48

The customer can then divide it into multiple /64 LAN prefixes:

LAN 1
2001:db8:2000:1::/64

LAN 2
2001:db8:2000:2::/64

LAN 3
2001:db8:2000:3::/64

The ISP would normally maintain reachability toward the customer's allocated prefix rather than only a single /128 Loopback.

Production environments may also use:

OSPFv3
IS-IS for IPv6
eBGP
iBGP
IPv6 address-family
Prefix filtering
Route policies
BGP communities
Customer prefix delegation
Dual-stack deployment
